<ReportItem port="111" svc_name="rpc-portmapper" protocol="udp" severity="0" pluginID="10223" pluginName="RPC portmapper Service Detection" pluginFamily="RPC">
<cve>CVE-1999-0632</cve>
<cvss3_base_score>0.0</cvss3_base_score>
<cvss3_vector>CVSS:3.0/AV:N/AC:L/PR:N/UI:N/S:U/C:N/I:N/A:N</cvss3_vector>
<cvss_base_score>0.0</cvss_base_score>
<cvss_score_source>CVE-1999-0632</cvss_score_source>
<cvss_vector>CVSS2#AV:N/AC:L/Au:N/C:N/I:N/A:N</cvss_vector>
<description>The RPC portmapper is running on this port.

The portmapper allows someone to get the port number of each RPC service running on the remote host by sending either multiple lookup requests or a DUMP request.</description>
<fname>rpc_portmap.nasl</fname>
<plugin_modification_date>2019/10/04</plugin_modification_date>
<plugin_name>RPC portmapper Service Detection</plugin_name>
<plugin_publication_date>1999/08/19</plugin_publication_date>
<plugin_type>remote</plugin_type>
<risk_factor>None</risk_factor>
<script_version>1.37</script_version>
<solution>n/a</solution>
<synopsis>An ONC RPC portmapper is running on the remote host.</synopsis>
</ReportItem>
<ReportItem port="2049" svc_name="rpc-status" protocol="tcp" severity="0" pluginID="11111" pluginName="RPC Services Enumeration" pluginFamily="Service detection">
<description>By sending a DUMP request to the portmapper, it was possible to enumerate the ONC RPC services running on the remote port.  Using this information, it is possible to connect and bind to each service by sending an RPC request to the remote port.</description>
<fname>rpcinfo.nasl</fname>
<plugin_modification_date>2011/05/24</plugin_modification_date>
