<ReportItem port="2049" svc_name="rpc-nfs_acl" protocol="tcp" severity="0" pluginID="10437" pluginName="NFS Share Export List" pluginFamily="RPC">
<cvss_score_source>CVE-1999-0554</cvss_score_source>
<description>This plugin retrieves the list of NFS exported shares.</description>
<fname>showmount.nasl</fname>
<plugin_modification_date>2019/10/04</plugin_modification_date>
<plugin_name>NFS Share Export List</plugin_name>
<plugin_publication_date>2000/06/07</plugin_publication_date>
<plugin_type>remote</plugin_type>
<risk_factor>None</risk_factor>
<script_version>1.36</script_version>
<see_also>http://www.tldp.org/HOWTO/NFS-HOWTO/security.html</see_also>
<solution>Ensure each share is intended to be exported.</solution>
<synopsis>The remote NFS server exports a list of shares.</synopsis>
<plugin_output>
Here is the export list of 10.114.132.23 :
  
  /export/anonymous 
  /shared 
</plugin_output>
</ReportItem>
<ReportItem port="2049" svc_name="rpc-nfs_acl" protocol="udp" severity="3" pluginID="15984" pluginName="NFS Share User Mountable" pluginFamily="RPC">
<cvss3_base_score>7.3</cvss3_base_score>
<cvss3_vector>CVSS:3.0/AV:N/AC:L/PR:N/UI:N/S:U/C:L/I:L/A:L</cvss3_vector>
<cvss_base_score>7.5</cvss_base_score>
<cvss_vector>CVSS2#AV:N/AC:L/Au:N/C:P/I:P/A:P</cvss_vector>
<description>Nessus was either able to mount some of the NFS shares exported by the remote server or disclose potentially sensitive information such as a directory listing. An attacker may exploit this issue to gain read and possibly write access to files on remote host.

Note that root privileges were not required to mount the remote shares since the source port to mount the shares was higher than 1024.</description>
<fname>nfs_user_mount.nasl</fname>
<plugin_modification_date>2018/08/01</plugin_modification_date>
<plugin_name>NFS Share User Mountable</plugin_name>
<plugin_publication_date>2004/12/16</plugin_publication_date>
<plugin_type>remote</plugin_type>
<risk_factor>High</risk_factor>
<script_version>1.14</script_version>
<solution>Configure NFS on the remote host so that only authorized hosts can mount the remote shares. The remote NFS server should prevent mount requests originating from a non-privileged port.</solution>
<synopsis>Nessus was able to access sensitive information from remote NFS shares without having root privileges.</synopsis>
<plugin_output>
Potentially sensitive information was able to be obtained from the
following shares without root privileges :

+ /export/anonymous
  + Contents of /export/anonymous : 
    - .
    - ..
    - public_info.txt
</plugin_output>
</ReportItem>
<ReportItem port="161" svc_name="snmp" protocol="udp" severity="0" pluginID="35296" pluginName="SNMP Protocol Version Detection" pluginFamily="SNMP">
<asset_inventory>True</asset_inventory>
