<ReportItem port="23" svc_name="telnet" protocol="tcp" severity="2" pluginID="42263" pluginName="Unencrypted Telnet Server" pluginFamily="Misc.">
<cvss3_base_score>6.5</cvss3_base_score>
<cvss3_vector>CVSS:3.0/AV:N/AC:L/PR:N/UI:N/S:U/C:L/I:L/A:N</cvss3_vector>
<cvss_base_score>5.8</cvss_base_score>
<cvss_score_rationale>Information disclosure vulnerability.</cvss_score_rationale>
<cvss_score_source>manual</cvss_score_source>
<cvss_vector>CVSS2#AV:N/AC:M/Au:N/C:P/I:P/A:N</cvss_vector>
<description>The remote host is running a Telnet server over an unencrypted channel.

Using Telnet over an unencrypted channel is not recommended as logins, passwords, and commands are transferred in cleartext. This allows a remote, man-in-the-middle attacker to eavesdrop on a Telnet session to obtain credentials or other sensitive information and to modify traffic exchanged between a client and server.

SSH is preferred over Telnet since it protects credentials from eavesdropping and can tunnel additional data streams such as an X11 session.</description>
<fname>telnet_clear_text.nasl</fname>
<plugin_modification_date>2024/01/16</plugin_modification_date>
<plugin_name>Unencrypted Telnet Server</plugin_name>
<plugin_publication_date>2009/10/27</plugin_publication_date>
<plugin_type>remote</plugin_type>
<risk_factor>Medium</risk_factor>
<script_version>1.15</script_version>
<solution>Disable the Telnet service and use SSH instead.</solution>
<synopsis>The remote Telnet server transmits traffic in cleartext.</synopsis>
<plugin_output>
Nessus collected the following banner from the remote Telnet server :

------------------------------ snip ------------------------------
User Access Verification
login: 
------------------------------ snip ------------------------------
</plugin_output>
</ReportItem>
